NAME
       cloudctl - the command line for Skyloft Cloud, a pretend cloud (Cyber Range)

SYNOPSIS
       cloudctl configure KEY SECRET
       cloudctl whoami
       cloudctl [--anonymous] storage ls [BUCKET[/FOLDER]]
       cloudctl [--anonymous] storage get BUCKET/PATH [-o FILE]
       cloudctl storage policy BUCKET
       cloudctl iam show [NAME] | iam roles | iam assume ROLE
       cloudctl secrets ls | secrets get NAME
       cloudctl network groups
       cloudctl compute ls

DESCRIPTION
       Skyloft Cloud is a made-up cloud provider that exists only inside Cyber
       Range. Like the real ones, it rents out storage buckets (folders of
       files on the internet), virtual machines, a store for secrets, and
       firewall rules called security groups. Who may do what is decided by
       identities and their permissions (IAM, "identity and access
       management").

       To use it you need credentials: an access KEY (a name such as
       SKY1A2B3C...) and its SECRET. cloudctl configure saves them in
       ~/.skyloft/credentials; every later command signs in with them. With no
       credentials (or --anonymous) you are an anonymous visitor, who can only
       look into buckets that were made public.

       The classic cloud mistakes you can practise: a bucket left public with a
       backup inside, keys left in a file anyone can read, an identity allowed
       to take on a much stronger role, a server whose "fetch this address"
       feature reaches the cloud's metadata address 169.254.169.254 (which
       hands out the server's own credentials), and a security group that lets
       the whole internet (0.0.0.0/0) reach a port.

COMMANDS
       configure KEY SECRET    save the credentials
       whoami                  your identity and what it may do
       storage ls              the buckets you may list (needs credentials)
       storage ls BUCKET       the files in a bucket (public ones: anyone)
       storage get BUCKET/PATH print a file (-o FILE saves it)
       storage policy BUCKET   whether a bucket is public
       iam show [NAME]         an identity's permissions (others need iam:read)
       iam roles               the roles (needs iam:list)
       iam assume ROLE         temporary credentials for a role (needs iam:assume)
       secrets ls / get NAME   the secret store (secrets:list, secrets:read)
       network groups          the security groups (network:list)
       compute ls              the virtual machines (compute:list)

PERMISSIONS
       Each permission is an action and a resource, for example
       storage:read app-logs, secrets:read prod/*, iam:assume deployer. A * in
       the resource matches anything, so secrets:read * may read every
       secret - usually far more than an identity needs.

EXAMPLES
           cloudctl --anonymous storage ls riverbend-backups
           cloudctl storage get riverbend-backups/db/2026-10-01.sql
           cloudctl configure SKY4F2A9C0D1E SECRETTEXT
           cloudctl whoami
           cloudctl iam assume deployer

